  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FND/Utilities/Analytics/Median">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FND/Utilities/Analytics/StatisticalMeasure"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://www.omg.org/spec/Commons/QuantitiesAndUnits/Expression"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="Ndc8766e63b6e40a9bf67ef4f7dc25ae7"/>
    <rdfs:label>median</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition>value of the variate dividing the total frequency of a data sample, population, or probability distribution, into two halves</skos:definition>
    <cmns-av:adaptedFrom rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">https://stats.oecd.org/glossary/detail.asp?ID=3717</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
    <cmns-av:explanatoryNote>The basic advantage of the median in describing data compared to the mean is that it is not skewed by extremely large or small values, and may provide a better idea of a 'typical' value.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
    <cmns-av:explanatoryNote>This measure represents the middle value (if n is odd) or the average of the two middle values (if n is even) in an ordered list of data values. The median divides the total frequency distribution into two equal parts: one-half of the cases fall below the median and one-half of the cases exceed the median.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
  </rdf:Description>
