  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FBC/FunctionalEntities/FinancialServicesEntities/BrokerageFirm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FBC/FunctionalEntities/FinancialServicesEntities/NonDepositoryInstitution"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.edmcouncil.org/fibo/ontology/FBC/ProductsAndServices/FinancialProductsAndServices/BrokerDealer"/>
    <rdfs:label>brokerage firm</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition>firm in the business of buying and selling securities, operating as both a broker and a dealer, depending on the transaction</skos:definition>
    <fibo-fnd-utl-av:definitionOrigin>Office of Financial Research (OFR) Annual Report, 2012, Glossary</fibo-fnd-utl-av:definitionOrigin>
    <cmns-av:adaptedFrom rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/gfdr/gfdr-2016/background/nonbank-financial-institution</cmns-av:adaptedFrom>
    <cmns-av:explanatoryNote>The term broker-dealer is used in U.S. securities regulation parlance to describe stock brokerages, because most of them act as both agents and principals. A brokerage acts as a broker (or agent) when it executes orders on behalf of clients, whereas it acts as a dealer (or principal) when it trades for its own account.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
    <cmns-av:synonym>market maker</cmns-av:synonym>
  </rdf:Description>
